This repository contains my experimentation with interpreted machine language.

I am slowly collecting 15+ years of software and data into this repo, so expect
it to grow as time goes by.

Programs contained herein:

--- Client program: the console ---

NOTE: "console" has been superceded by "fconsole", which uses a client side
frame buffer to render the screen instead of drawing directly on the server
side drawables. This cuts down on XWindows overhead IMMENSELY and pushes
framerates into the 60-120/sec range.

console (fconsole) - This is the IML console. It contains the video (and eventually audio)
frontend and memory/IO structure for any fictitious microprocessor that would
like to use it.

command switches for console:

-s (size scale): embiggens the console window by a factor of size scale.
Default is 2.

-e (server): automatically loads up and fork/execs any of the programs listed
below. This is a timesaver so you don't have to open the server in a different
window.

example: ./console -s3 -e./randvideo

tconsole - Text console for a terminal, no X11 needed. It prints the characters
the server writes to the text console (IO_CON_REGISTER and IO_CON_CR) on stdout
as they are written, in video modes 8 and 9. Modes 0-7 show nothing, and the
cursor position and clear screen soft switches are ignored. Keys are sent like
fconsole sends them (Enter = 13, Backspace = 8, arrows = 8/9/10/11); Ctrl-Alt-End
is a warm reset and Ctrl-C quits (Ctrl-Z is passed on as a key: it's BASIC's break key).
Input can also be piped in.

example: ./tconsole -e ./m68k

tconsole connects with IO_CMD_CLIENTALIVE byte = 1, which tells the server's IO
driver to send every console character as an IO_CMD_CONCHAR ($F903) message. The
X11 consoles connect with byte = 0 and get no such messages.

--- Utilities ---

bsave - Save a block of shared memory to a file, while a server is running.
bload - Load a file into shared memory at an address, while a server is running.

usage: ./bsave --address <addr> --length <len> filename
       ./bload --address <addr> filename

Addresses and lengths are decimal, or hex with an h or H suffix (e.g. 1c0000h).
Short forms -a and -l also work. The block must fit inside the 2MB shared memory
segment. Like the other programs, run them from the IML directory (they find
shared memory through the "token" file). They attach to the existing segment and never create
one, so if no server is running they just say so and exit. bload writes straight
into memory, bypassing the soft switches and the ROM write protection.

example: ./bsave -a 1c0000h -l 1024 vectors.bin
         ./bload -a 10000h prog.bin

gconvert - Convert a BMP, Targa, JPEG or PNG image to a 4096 color screen image.

usage: ./gconvert -i input.(bmp/tga/jpg/png) -o screen.scr -m (4/5/6) [-s]
       (long forms: --input --output --mode --scale)

Mode 4 is 120x68, 5 is 240x136 and 6 is 480x272. The output is the raw video
memory for that mode, to be loaded at 180000h: one 16 bit pixel per position, red
in bits 8-11, green 4-7, blue 0-3, stored low byte first (the way the consoles
read it). An image smaller than the screen is centered on black. An image bigger
than the screen is an error unless -s is given; -s scales the image up or down to
fit, keeping its aspect ratio, and fills the rest of the screen with black.

Supported: BMP (1/4/8 bit palette, RLE4/RLE8, 16/24/32 bit), Targa (palette,
truecolor, grayscale, plain or RLE), baseline JPEG and PNG (all color types and
bit depths, interlaced or not; transparent pixels are blended onto black).
Progressive JPEGs aren't supported (e.g. "magick in.jpg -interlace none out.jpg"
makes a baseline one). Images can be at most 4096x4096. gconvert needs zlib
(zlib1g-dev) to build.

example: ./gconvert -i photo.jpg -o photo.scr -m 6 -s
         ./bload -a 180000h photo.scr   (with a server running in mode 6)

--- Server programs ---

65816 - This is my 65816 emulator. It loads up a ROM file called "e4.o" at
$00/E400 and this contains a mock ROM and all the ROM vectors up to $00/FFFF.
It initiates a reset cycle, which temporarily just prints a ROM splash message
and then executes a STP instruction, halting the processor.

randvideo - This cycles through the console's video modes and throws random
information up on the screen, cycling every second.

kbdecho - Demonstrates keyboard echo using the built in CON feature of the
IO layer.

m68k - Motorola 68000 emulator. It loads a ROM file called "mrom.bin" at
$1C0000 (the write protected 1C ROM area) and copies the ROM's first 1K, its
exception vector table, down to $000000, where the 68000 fetches its reset
vectors. The vector table is RAM, so programs can hook exceptions. Ctrl-Alt-End
copies the vectors again and resets the CPU. The 68000 sees the 2MB space flat
(no Z80 windows): the soft switches are at $1BFC00.

The ROM (mrom.s) is a machine language monitor with a disassembler and an
Apple II style mini-assembler; type ? for its commands. It is built with vasm
(Motorola syntax): the Makefile runs "vasm.vasmm68k-mot" (the snap package's
name), override with "make VASM=vasmm68k_mot" if yours is called differently.
All numbers are hex, so a TRAP is "TRAP #$F" or "TRAP #0F".

BASIC (basic.s, built into basic.bin by the same make) is loaded at the next $100
boundary after the monitor ($1C2A00 at the moment); the Makefile works the address
out from the size of mrom.bin, and m68k checks that basic.bin was built for it. In
the monitor, B starts BASIC (cold start) and W goes back to it keeping the program
(warm start); MONITOR in BASIC returns to the monitor. See BASIC_README for the
language.

68000 memory map:

00/0000-00/03FF - Exception vectors (RAM, copied from the ROM at reset)
00/0400-00/0FFF - Monitor variables
00/1000-00/1003 - BASIC: the address of its memory block (LOMEM)
00/1004-00/FFFF - Supervisor stack (starts at 01/0000)
01/0000-17/FFFF - User RAM (BASIC uses 01/0000-16/FFFF by default)
18/0000-1B/FBFF - Video buffer
1B/FC00-1B/FFFF - Soft switches
1C/0000-1F/FFFF - ROM: vector table, the monitor at 1C/0400, then BASIC

Programs started with G run in supervisor mode and can return with RTS. Any
exception prints its name and the registers (R shows them again) and returns
to the monitor. TRAP #15 is a system call with the function number in D0.B:
0 putc D1.B, 1 getc -> D1.B, 2 puts (A1), 3/4/5 print D1 as a hex byte/word/
long, 6 newline, 7 getline -> A1, 8 key waiting -> D1.B (0 = none), 9 return
to the monitor.

--- Technical details about IML environment ---

Memory map:

00/0000-00/00FF - Zero page. Not used on processors other than 65 series.
00/0100-00/01FF - Emulation mode stack. Not used on processors other than 65 series.
00/0200-00/E3FF - Bank 0 program area, native mode stack starts at E3FF and works down.
00/E400-00/FFFF - 7k Bank 0 ROM image, reset vectors
01/0000-16/FFFF - Roughly 1.5MB of user RAM area
17/0000-17/FFFF - 1C ROM data area, system usage
18/0000-1B/FBFF - 256k video buffer
1B/FC00-1B/FFFF - Soft switches (see guide below)
1C/0000-1F/FFFF - 256k 1C ROM image
20/0000-FF/FFFF - 14MB free RAM area for future expansion, currently unimplemented on 2MB system.

Soft switches:

IO_KEYQ_SIZE = 0x1bfc00 - (read) - Number of keypresses waiting in keyboard queue
IO_KEYQ_WAITING = 0x1bfc01 - (read) The next waiting keypress
IO_KEYQ_DEQUEUE = 0x1bfc02 - (write any value) - Remove longest keypress, reveal next waiting keypress
IO_KEYQ_CLEAR = 0x1bfc03 - (write any value) - Clear keyboard queue
IO_KEYQ_FIND = 0x1bfc04 - (write a key code) - Remove the first of that key from anywhere in the queue (other keys stay in order)
IO_KEYQ_FOUND = 0x1bfc05 - (read) - 1 if IO_KEYQ_FIND found the key, 0 if not
IO_CON_CLS = 0x1bfc10 - (write any value) - Clear the text screen (video modes 8/9) to CHAROUT using COLOR
IO_CON_COLOR = 0x1bfc11 - (read/write) - Set color byte to be used on next CLS or REGISTER operation
IO_CON_CHAROUT = 0x1bfc12 - (read/write) - Character to use for next CLS or REGISTER operation
IO_CON_REGISTER = 0x1bfc13 - (write any value) - Writes CHAROUT to screen, advances cursors/scrolls screen if necessary
IO_CON_CURSORH = 0x1bfc14 - (read/write) - Horizontal cursor position
IO_CON_CURSORV = 0x1bfc15 - (read/write) - Vertizontal cursor position
IO_CON_CURSOR = 0x1bfc16 - (read/write) - High bit set determines if cursor is visible
IO_CON_CR = 0x1bfc17 - (write any value) - Prints a carriage return
IO_VIDMODE = 0x1bfc20 - (read/write) - Console video mode

Floating point: FACC (0x1bfce0) and FARG (0x1bfcf0) are the two registers, FPINT
(0x1bfcd8) is a 64 bit integer (little endian), FPASCII (0x1bfcc0) is a text buffer
and FPCOND (0x1bfcbf) reports results. Writing a command byte to one of these
starts the operation: bit 7 = 0 float / 1 double, bit 5 = 1 Intel 80 bit (then
bit 7 doesn't matter), bit 6 = 0 FACC / 1 FARG (the register used, or written back
to), bits 0-4 = more data for the command.

IO_FP_INIT_CONSTANT = 0x1bfc40 - load a constant (bits 0-4: 0-5, 10, 100, -1..-5, -10, pi, e, sqrt 2, phi)
IO_FP_TO_ASCII = 0x1bfc41 - register to FPASCII, fixed point
IO_FP_MULTIPLY/DIVIDE/ADD/SUBTRACT = 0x1bfc42-0x1bfc45 - FACC op FARG (divide by zero: FPCOND bit 7)
IO_FP_LN = 0x1bfc46, IO_FP_EXP = 0x1bfc49 - natural log, e to the power, in place
IO_FP_ILOAD = 0x1bfc47 - FPINT to the register
IO_FP_ISAVE = 0x1bfc48 - the register's integer part to FPINT
IO_FP_SIN/COS/TAN/ATN = 0x1bfc4a-0x1bfc4d - in place, radians
IO_FP_POWER = 0x1bfc4e - FACC ^ FARG
IO_FP_COMPARE = 0x1bfc4f - FACC vs FARG -> FPCOND: 1 equal, 2 less, 4 greater, 8 unordered
IO_FP_FROM_ASCII = 0x1bfc50 - text at FPASCII (up to 31 characters) to the register; FPCOND bit 6 if it isn't a number
IO_FP_TO_ASCII_G = 0x1bfc51 - the register to FPASCII in %g style (bits 0-4 = significant digits, 0 = 18), up to 32 bytes
IO_FP_FUNC = 0x1bfc52 - in place, bits 0-4: 0 log10, 1 sqrt, 2 abs, 3 negate, 4 floor, 5 ceiling, 6 truncate, 7 round, 8 arcsin, 9 arccos
IO_FP_CLASSIFY = 0x1bfc53 - the register -> FPCOND: 1 zero, 2 negative, $10 not a number, $20 infinite
IO_FP_ISAVE_CHECKED = 0x1bfc54 - like ISAVE, but FPCOND bit 5 (and 0) if it doesn't fit in 64 bits
IO_FP_TO_ASCII_F = 0x1bfc55 - the register to FPASCII in fixed point, bits 0-4 = digits after the point (too long for 32 bytes: "" and FPCOND bit 5)
IO_FP_TO_ASCII_E = 0x1bfc56 - the same with an exponent (1.23E+03)

Video modes:

0 - 120x68 Low-res 16 colors (1 nibble per pixel)
1 - 240x136 Medium-res 16 colors
2 - 480x272 High-res 16 colors
4 - 120x68 Low-res 4096 colors (2 bytes per pixel, low 12 bits of 16 bit word are significant)
5 - 240x136 Medium-res 4096 colors
6 - 480x272 High-res 4096 colors
8 - 40x17 Text, 16 colors, 16 background colors
9 - 80x34 Text, 16 colors, 16 background colors

